**8. Exception Handling in Tests** (Core Smell #8)
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError` · Lines 110–118

```csharp
try
{
    processor.ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.Fail("Expected an exception but none was thrown");
}
catch (ValidationException ex)
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```

**Problem:** Manual try/catch instead of MSTest's built-in `Assert.ThrowsException<T>`. The pattern works (capture-and-assert), but is verbose and error-prone.

**Fix:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order();

    var ex = Assert.ThrowsException<ValidationException>(
        () => processor.ProcessOrder(order));

    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** If someone removes the `Assert.Fail`, the test silently passes on the happy path.

